## 1. Introduction
Semiconductor photocatalysis, an environmentally friendly technology utilizing clear and recyclable solar energy, has been recently receiving widespread attention in a variety of valuable chemical reactions [1–8]. As we know, a whole photocatalytic reaction consists of three processes [9,10]. The light-harvesting center is initially excited by the incident photon to yield electron–hole pairs. Subsequently, photogenerated electrons and holes are separated and transported from light-harvesting center to active site. At last, the target molecule would be chemisorbed and activated at corresponding active site, accomplishing the surface redox reactions. Based on this, the light-harvesting center and the active site are two main components in photocatalyst systems [9]. For heterogeneous photocatalysis, semiconductors usually behave as the light-harvesting centers. Furthermore, plasmonic metals such as Au and Cu can also act as the light-harvesting centers because of localized surface plasmon resonance (LSPR) [11]. The plasmonmediated photocatalysts would show an enhanced light harvesting and a lower recombination rate of photogenerated carriers than their counterparts. The plasmonic hot electrons could facilitate the surface redox reactions by an injection to the conduction band of photocatalysts. Therefore, loading plasmonic metals is an effective strategy to improve the photocatalytic performance [12]. Constructing active sites will improve the activation of reagent molecules for achieving high performance and high selectivity [13]. However, developing appropriate catalytic sites is still a basic question for traditional semiconductor photocatalyst.
Metal organic frameworks (MOF), a novel kind of functional porous material, have aroused more and more attention in recent years [14]. The unique properties of MOF, such as superhigh specific surface area, tunable functionality and ample active site, enable them to become versatile materials for energy storage and conversion, heterogeneous catalysis, gas separation, biomedicine and sensing [15–23]. MOF as characteristic catalysts can not only make efficient use of active sites (the superiority of homogeneous catalysts) but also have the excellent recyclability (the merit of heterogeneous catalysts) [16]. Meanwhile, charge carriers can transport in MOF by the photoexcitation of metal cluster or organic linker, behaving as semiconducting materials [24]. Being different from traditional semiconductors, the structure and functionality of MOF are feasibly modulated by changing metal center or the organic linker, providing an opportunity to design photocatalysts at the molecular level. Therefore, MOF could be a suitable platform to develop the excellent heterogeneous photocatalysts.
There are many excellent reviews in the literatures about designing MOF-based photocatalysts [25–27] The strategies mainly include the ligand functionalization [28–30], the sensitization [31,32], the combination with metal nanoparticles (NPs) or other semiconductors [15,33–36] and the introduction of defects [37]. By the grafting of various ligand substitutions (such as - $\mathsf{N H}_{2},\mathsf{C H}_{3},\mathsf{N O}_{2}),$ , the ligand functionalization can improve the visible light absorption and make electronic effects on MOF photocatalysts [28–30]. Using photosensitizers (organometallic complexes and dyes) can be a useful method for enhancing absorption strength of light [31,32]. The decoration of precious metal nanoparticles can boost the photocatalytic activity due to forming excited electrons via the LSPR and promoting the separation of photogenerated carriers by trapping electrons [15]. In addition, defect engineering is also a novel strategy to modulate band gap and electronic properties of MOFs [37]. By introducing defects into MOFs, more active catalytically sites such as Lewis acid sites will be formed, and the diffusion limitations can be partially solved [38].
Imine compounds, a class of valuable intermediates, can behave as the electrophilic reagent for many organic synthesis reactions [39]. The visible-light-induced photocatalytic oxidation of amines to form imines has recently attracted wide attention because the reaction can proceed under mild condition and achieve high selectivity [40–43]. Herein, we successfully developed Au nanoparticles (NPs)-decorated defective DUT-67(Zr) containing oxygen vacancies $(\mathsf{A u@D U T-67(Z r)})$ via the photodeposition process for photocatalytic transformation of amines to imines under visible light. Morphology and structure for Au@DUT-67(Zr) were confirmed via scanning electron microscope (SEM), transmission electron microscope (TEM), energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) mappings and $\Nu_{2}$ sorption tests. The interaction between Au NPs and DUT-67 (Zr) was verified by X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectra. The forming and its mechanism of oxygen vacancies were studied by XPS, thermogravimetric analysis (TGA), electron spin resonance (ESR) spectra and Fourier transform infrared (FTIR)
spectra. In situ FTIR tests were used to explore the activation of benzylamine at catalyst. Generated superoxide radical $(\cdot0_{2}^{-})$ was revealed via 5,5-dimethyl-1-pyrroline $N\mathrm{\Omega}$ -oxide (DMPO)-combined ESR spectrum. Finally, the synergetic mechanism was presented to illustrate photocatalytic progress at the molecular level.
## 2. Experimental
2.1. Reagents.
Zirconium (IV) chloride $(\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}},98\ \%)$ is obtained from Aladdin Reagent Co., Ltd. 2,5-thiophenedicarboxylic acid $(\mathrm{H_{2}T D C},\ 99\ \%)$ terephthalic acid $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC},\ 99\ \%)$ , $N\mathrm{.}$ -methyl pyrrolidone (NMP, $99\ \%$ ), benzylamine (BA, $99\ \%$ and $N$ -benzyleneamine (BE, $99\ \%$ ) are bought from J&K Scientific ltd. N, $N$ -dimethylformamide (DMF, AR), chloroauric acid $\mathrm{(HAuCl_{4}}$ , AR), acetic acid $\mathrm{\langleCH_{3}C O O H}$ AR), methanol $\mathrm{\cdot}C H_{3}O H$ , AR), sodium sulfate $(\mathsf{N a}_{2}\mathsf{S O}_{4}$ , AR), potassium chloride (KCl, AR), potassium ferricyanide $(\mathrm{K}_{3}\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}$ , AR) and potassium hexacyanoferrate(II) $({\mathrm{K}}_{4}{\mathrm{Fe}}({\mathrm{CN}})_{6}$ , AR) are purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. The above reagents are used without a further purification.
2.2. Catalyst preparation.
## 2.2.1. DUT-67(Zr)
DUT- $67(\mathrm{Zr})$ sample is prepared via a modulated synthesis approach based on the report by Kaskel et al [44]. Firstly, $230~\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathrm{ZrCl}_{4}$ is added into $25~\mathrm{mL}$ of mixed solution (The volume ratio of DMF and NMP is 1: 1) under constantly stirring to ensure complete dissolution. Then, $110\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{T D C}$ and $7\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O H}$ are successively added under stirring for $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ . Finally, the obtained solution is decanted into an autoclave and heated in an oven at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ . After cooled naturally, the precipitate is washed five times using the mixture of DMF and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ and then dried in vacuum at $100^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
## 2.2.2. Au@DUT-67(Zr)
Au nanoparticles-decorated DUT-67 $(\mathrm{zr})$ , denoted as Au@DUT$67(\mathrm{Zr})$ , is prepared via a facile room-temperature photodepositioon method. Firstly, $100~\mathrm{{mg}}$ of DUT- $.67(\mathrm{Zr})$ is dispersed into water and then sonicated for 5 min to obtain the colloidal solution. Then, a given amount of $\mathrm{\thAuCl_{4}}$ aqueous solution is injected in the solution with passing $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ for $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ . The above solution is irradiated by the Xenon lamp (Beijing Perfectlight, PLS-SXE300D) for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ . The final sample is washed several times using water, and dried in vacuum at $100~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ By varying dosage of $\mathsf{H A u C l}_{4}$ Au@DUT-67(Zr) samples with different mass fraction $0.5\ \%1\ \%$ $1.5\%$ and $2\%$ ) of Au are prepared using the above procedure and labeled as 0.5 %Au@DUT-67(Zr), 1 %Au@DUT-67(Zr), 1.5 $\%$ $\mathsf{A u@D U T-67(Z r)}$ and 2 %Au@DUT-67(Zr). In most cases, $\mathsf{A u@}$ DUT$67(\mathrm{Zr})$ is regarded as 1 %Au@DUT-67(Zr).
## 2.2.3. UiO- $66(Z r)$ and Au@UiO-66(Zr)
UiO-66 is prepared via a modified solvothermal process [45]. Firstly, $\mathrm{\zr{Cl_{4}}}$ $\cdot230{\mathrm{~mg}},$ and $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{B D C}$ ( $161~\mathrm{mg},$ are successively dissolved in DMF $(50~\mathrm{mL}$ and constantly stirred to ensure complete dissolution. The above solution is heated in an autoclave at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The final sample is washed several times using $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ and DMF and dried in vacuum at $100~^{\circ}\mathsf C$ for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Au@UiO-66 is prepared by the same photodeposition procedure as 1 %Au@DUT-67(Zr).
## 2.3. Photocatalytic activity tests
The photocatalytic aerobic oxidation of BA is proceeded as follows. First, the catalyst $(20~\mathrm{mg})$ , BA $\mathrm{(0.20~mmol)}$ , DMF $(1.0~\mathrm{mL})$ are mixed in the reaction tube (Fig. S1). The mixture is constantly stirred with oxygen passing for $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ . Then, the tube is irradiated via the 300 W Xe lamp with a $400\ \mathrm{nm}$ cut-off filter. During irradiation, the thermostat bath with circulating water is installed for keeping the temperature $(25^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ . Six hours later, the reactive solution is obtained by centrifugation and detected by a Shimadzu Gas Chromatograph (GC-2014C). The conversion of BA and the selectivity of BE are illustrated as follows:
Conversion $\left(\%\right)=\left[\left(\mathsf{C}_{0}-\mathsf{C}_{\mathrm{BA}}\right)/\mathsf{C}_{0}\right]\times100.$
Selectivity $(\%)\ =\ [2C_{\mathrm{BE}}\ /\ (C_{0}{-}C_{\mathrm{BA}})]\ \times\ 100.$ .
where ${\sf C}_{0}$ is the initial concentration of BA.
## 2.4. In situ FTIR tests
In situ FTIR tests were processed on a Nicolet IS50 Fourier transform infrared spectrometer. Each spectrum was collected by a total of 64 scans with a resolution of $4\:\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . Firstly, the mixed powder ( $\mathrm{\Delta}5\mathrm{\mg}$ catalyst $+15{\mathrm{~mg~KBr}}$ ) was pressed into a self-supporting IR disk whose diameter is $18~\mathrm{mm}$ , and then the disk was put into a holder which could be moved vertically along a quartz tube of the cell. The sample disk was treated under dynamic vacuum $(5\times10^{-2}~\mathrm{hPa})$ at $120^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ for $^{3\mathrm{~h~}}$ to remove absorbed gas before starting the first FTIR test (denoted as degassing stage). After the cell was cooled to room temperature, $20~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of benzylamine (BA) was injected into the cell using a syringe via the septum. Half an hour later, the second FTIR test (denoted as physical and chemical adsorption stage) was processed. The physically absorbed BA was removed by a further evacuation at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $5\ \mathrm{min}$ under $5\times10^{-2}$ hPa, and then the third FTIR measurement (denoted as chemical adsorption stage) was processed.
## 2.5. Catalyst characterization
The details of characterization and electrochemistry tests are illustrated in the support information.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Catalyst characterization
In the powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns (Fig. 1A), DUT67(Zr) sample shows clear diffraction peaks, in keeping with the calculated data [46]. It can also be found that these peaks are sharp and intense, suggesting the excellent crystallinity for DUT-67(Zr) sample. Au@DUT-67(Zr) exhibits similar XRD patterns to that of DUT-67(Zr), indicating the integral structure for DUT-67(Zr) is kept. However, no typical peak of Au is observed for Au@DUT-67 (Zr), which might be contributed to the high distribution and low loading [33]. In Fig. 1B, DUT-67(Zr) and Au@DUT-67(Zr) both possess the type I isotherm with a H4-type hysteresis loop, revealing they belong to microporous materials with some mesoporous. Based on calculated results using Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) theory, the specific surface area of Au@DUT-67(Zr) is $415.6~\mathrm{m}^{3}/\mathrm{g},$ which slightly decreases compared with that $(490.1~\mathrm{~m}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ of DUT-67(Zr). The pore size distribution curves (Fig. 1C) demonstrate that their pore sizes are mainly distributed in $0.6\ \mathrm{nm}$ to $2.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ (microporous) and less range from $2.0\mathrm{nm}$ to $65.0\mathrm{nm}$ (mesoporous and macropore). The presence of mesoporous in MOFs would facilitate the mass transfer and the functional molecule encapsulation [47]. The total pore volume value $(0.2476~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ of Au@DUT-67 (Zr) is lower than that $(0.2887~\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ of DUT-67 $(\mathrm{Zr})$ , indicating that these pores might be blocked by the loaded Au NPs and thus the specific surface area decreases after the decoration of Au NPs.
The SEM image of DUT-67(Zr) in Fig. 2A clearly shows that it possesses a perfect polyhedron morphology. Interestingly, the high-angle annular dark-field scanning transmission electron microscope (HAADF-STEM) images at different angles (Fig. 2B) of DUT-67(Zr) show its partially hollow structure with enlarged channels. The SEM image (Fig. 2C) of Au@DUT-67(Zr) illustrates that the typical morphology of DUT-67(Zr) can be maintained after the photodeposition process. The HAADF-STEM images at different angles (Fig. 2D) of Au@DUT-67(Zr) show that Au NPs are effectively immobilized on DUT-67(Zr). The EDX mappings for Fig. 2E exhibit an overlay of C, O, S and Zr with partial vacancies, further confirming the hollow structure of DUT-67(Zr). The overlay of Au has no vacancy, suggesting Au NPs are evenly distributed on the whole DUT-67 $(Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{r}})$ structure. In addition, Fig. S2 further verifies that the size distribution of Au NPs mainly ranges from $15\ \mathrm{nm}$ to $20~\mathrm{{nm}}$ .
For comparison, the morphology and structure of $\mathsf{A u@U i O–66}$ (Zr) were also characterized. The SEM image in Fig. S3A exhibits the octahedral structure of $\mathsf{A u@U i O–66(Z r)}$ . The HAADF-STEM image in Fig. S3B further shows no vacancy in its structure and the less dispersion of Au NPs. An overlay of C, O and Zr in the $\mathsf{A u@U i O–66(Z r)}$ is shown in the EDX mappings in Fig. S3C, further confirming its intact structure. An overlay of Au illustrates the less distribution of Au NPs on the UiO-66(Zr) than that on the DUT-67 $(Z\mathbf{r})$ . Furthermore, the content for metal Au was further revealed by inductively coupled plasma (ICP) in Table S1. For Au@DUT-67(Zr), the Au content from the ICP result $(1.01\%)$ closes to the theoretical value $(1.00\mathrm{~\%})$ . However, the experimental value of Au content $(0.24\%)$ for Au@UiO-66(Zr) is obviously lower than the theoretical value. Therefore, it can be concluded that Au NPs can more effectively be dispersed on DUT- $.67(\mathrm{Zr})$ by the photodeposition strategy.
For investigating the mutual effect of Au NPs and DUT-67 $(\mathrm{Zr})$ , XPS characterization was performed. For Fig. 3A, Zr 3d XPS spectrum of Au@DUT-67(Zr) with the peaks for $182.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ $(3\mathsf{d}_{5/2})$ and 184.9 eV $(3\mathsf{d}_{3/2})$ is identical to that of DUT-67 $\left({{Z}_{\Gamma}}\right)$ , suggesting the chemical state of Zr atoms could not be affected by loading gold. Similarly, O 1s XPS spectra of DUT- $67(\mathrm{Zr})$ and $\mathsf{A u@D U T-67(Z r)}$ in Fig. 3B both exhibit the four peaks at 530.2 eV, 531.6 eV, $532.0~\mathrm{eV}$ , and $533.0\ \mathrm{eV},$ which can be assigned to the lattice oxygens $\mathrm{(O_{Lat})}$ , the oxygens in $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{T D C}$ linkers $(0_{\mathrm{Lin}})$ , the absorbed water $({\cal O}_{\mathrm{H}20})$ , and the adsorbed oxygens $(0_{\mathsf{V}})$ near oxygen vacancies (VO), respectively [46]. Differently, the S 2p XPS spectrum (Fig. 3C) of Au@DUT-67(Zr) shifts to higher binding energy $164.2\ \mathrm{~eV}$ and $165.3\mathrm{eV},$ than that $164.0\mathrm{eV}$ and 165.1 eV) of DUT-67(Zr), indicating that the electron density for S atoms decreases. In addition, the Au 4f XPS spectrum of Au@DUT-67(Zr) in Fig. 3D shows a negative shift $(0.3{\mathrm{~eV}})$ relative to metallic Au, suggesting that the electron density for Au atoms increases. Therefore, it could be inferred that loading of Au NPs leads to redistribution of electron from S atom to Au atom based on coordination chemistry [9], which is probably the reason why Au NPs can effectively be dispersed on DUT-67 (Zr). In this photodeposition procedure, Au atoms can be effectively fixed on DUT-67 $(\boldsymbol{Z}\boldsymbol{\Gamma})$ via their coordination interaction.
It is shown in Fig. 3B that Au@DUT-67(Zr) possesses higher percentage $(15.2\ \%)$ of $0_{\vee}$ atoms than DUT-67(Zr) $(9.6~\%)$ ), revealing that more VO were in-situ formed in $\mathsf{A u@D U T-67(Z r)}$ during the photodeposition process. This result was also proved by ESR spectrum. In Fig. 4A, DUT-67(Zr) and $\mathsf{A u@D U T-67(Z r)}$ both exhibit the typical ESR signal of the single electrons nearby the VO, which is centred at $\ g=2.003$ [48]. The ESR signal intensity of Au@DUT-67 $(\boldsymbol{Z}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{r}})$ is stronger than that of DUT- $67(\mathrm{Zr})$ , indicating that more VO has been in-situ generated after the modification. Moreover, the VO signal of UiO-66(Zr) is also enhanced after the photodeposition.
Therefore, both Zr-MOFs in-situ form more VO in the photodeposition process. But how the VO defects are formed?
TGA as a common technique was used to investigate the structural defects of MOFs. In Fig. 4B, the last leftover is ascribed to $Z\mathrm{r}0_{2}$ , which is normalized to $100\%$ for the comparison. The first stage at $25\mathrm{-}100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and the second stage at $100{-}355^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ are attributed to the weight loss of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and the modulator, respectively. During the third stage from $355^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ to $475~^{\circ}C.$ the ligands entirely combust and the whole framework collapses. By continuing to raise the temperature, the residue can sequentially convert to $Z\mathrm{r}S_{2}$ and $Z\mathrm{r}0_{2}$ . Notably, the third stage ascribed to the ligand loss is always applied to reveal the degree of defects [37]. The content (L2) of ligand loss in $\mathsf{A u@D U T-67(Z r)}$ is lower than that (L1) in DUT-67 $(Z\Gamma)$ , suggesting $\mathsf{A u@D U T-67(Z r)}$ possesses more missing ligand defects. Fig. 3B also shows that the percentage $(50.5~\%)$ of $0_{\mathrm{Lin}}$ atoms for Au@DUT-67(Zr) is lower than that $(56.5\ \%)$ for DUT-67 $(Z\Gamma)$ . The reduced amounts of ligands in $\mathsf{A u@D U T-67(Z r)}$ might attribute to the partial decomposition of the linkers in the photodeposition process.
The result is further revealed by FTIR spectra. For Fig. 5, the intensity of main peaks for $\mathsf{A u@D U T-67(Z r)}$ is weaker than that for DUT-67(Zr), indicating that some $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{T D C}$ linkers may be consumed in this process. The peaks at $1578\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1394~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ can be ascribed to in-plane (d) and out-of-plane (c) bending vibrations of the carboxylate group $\left(-\mathsf{C O O}^{-}\right)$ , and the peak at $1531~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ belongs to $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ vibration (leftward enlarged view) [49,50]. Compared with the $-\mathsf{C O O}^{-}$ peaks in DUT-67(Zr), the peaks in Au@DUT-67(Zr) both have a slight shift, which might be due to the decarboxylation of linkers. However, the peak for $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ in Au@DUT-67(Zr) shows no shift. At lower wavenumbers, the four peaks at $769~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $686~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $652~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $474~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are associated to OH and CH bending, $\upmu_{3}–0$ stretching, $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ -(OC) stretching and (HO)-Zr-(OH) bending mode (rightward enlarged view), respectively. It is worth noting that the peak $655~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) for Zr-(OC) in $\mathsf{A u@D U T-67(Z r)}$ shifts to a higher wavenumber than that $(652~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}.$ ) in DUT-67(Zr), indicating that partial $Z\mathrm{{r}}$ –(CO) bonds might be broken under the irradiation. However, the other peaks show no move. These results demonstrate that UV light irradiation induces the decarboxylation and a further partial removal of the terminal ligands by breaking Zr–(OC) bonds, forming the VO and coordinatively unsaturated $Z\mathrm{r}$ atoms.
A possible mechanism for the photodeposition was depicted in Scheme 1. Firstly, DUT-67(Zr) is evenly dispersed into $\mathrm{\HAuCl_{4}}$ aqueous solution in $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere. Under UV light irradiation, DUT-67(Zr) is excited to form photogenerated electron-hole pairs. Photogenerated electrons reduces $\mathsf{H A u C l}_{4}$ to metallic Au NPs, which can effectively be fixed on DUT-67(Zr) via their coordination interaction. Photogenerated holes partially break Zr–(OC) bonds to remove the terminal ligands, forming VO and coordinatively unsaturated Zr atoms. This process makes good use of photogenerated electron-hole pairs, achieving not only the decoration of Au NPs but also the forming of defects on DUT-67(Zr).
The MottSchottky plots (Fig. S4) reveal flat-band potential for $\mathsf{A u@D U T-67(Z r)}$ is not affected by introducing Au NPs. Ultraviolet– visible diffuse reflectance spectra (UV–vis DRS) in Fig. 4C show that Au@DUT-67(Zr) exhibits the absorption with a broad LSPR band at $530\mathrm{nm}$ . The LSPR peak for Au@DUT-67(Zr) shows a clear plasmon redshift compared with that of colloidal Au NPs, which results from the larger refractive index of the DUT-67(Zr) support [51]. In addition, adsorption edges of DUT-67(Zr) and Au@DUT-67(Zr) both were located at $330~\mathrm{nm}$ , thus their band gap energy is calculated to $3.8{\mathrm{~eV}}.$ Photoluminescence (PL) spectrum for $\mathsf{A u@D U T-67(Z r)}$ in Fig. 4D exhibits much weaker intensity than that of naked DUT-67(Zr), providing the evidence that the lifetime of photogenerated charge carriers would be greatly prolonged after loading of Au NPs. For transient photocurrent response (Fig. 4E), Au@DUT-67 (Zr) shows enhanced photocurrent density compared with DUT-67 $(\mathrm{Zr})$ , suggesting separation efficiency of photoexcited electrons and holes has been enhanced due to the interaction between Au NPs and DUT-67 $\left({{Z}_{\Gamma}}\right)$ . For electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) in Fig. 4F, Au@DUT-67(Zr) has the smaller arc than DUT-67 $(\boldsymbol{Z}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{r}})$ , suggesting that the electron transfer from interior to interface has been facilitated. The above results confirmed that an effective interaction would exist between Au NPs and DUT-67(Zr).
## 3.2. Photocatalytic performance
The photocatalytic performance for catalysts was tested in the benzylamine (BA) oxidation reaction irradiated by visible light ( ${\bf\lambda}\mathrm{\hat{\Omega}}\mathrm{~\boldmath~\mathcal~{~\lambda~}~}\lambda\geq400~\mathrm{nm}$ ). For Table 1, the conversion of $10.0\%$ was achieved over DUT-67(Zr). However, Fig. 4C illustrates that DUT-67(Zr) merely responds to ultraviolet light $\langle\lambda\leq330\mathrm{nm}\rangle$ . After the adsorption of BA molecules on DUT-67 $(\boldsymbol{Z}\boldsymbol{\Gamma})$ , the property of light absorption for DUT-67(Zr) has been strengthened (BA/DUT-67(Zr)). Taking into account unsaturated Zr Atoms of DUT- $.67(\mathrm{Zr})$ as Lewis acid sites, we guess that the interaction of BA with DUT-67(Zr)
could facilitate forming surface coordination species, improving the absorption strength of visible light because of ligand-tometal charge transfer (LMCT) [52–54]. The conversion $(67.9\ \%)$ 1 with high selectivity $(99.1\ \%)$ was finished over Au@DUT-67(Zr), exhibiting more excellent performance than the naked DUT-67 $(Z\mathbf{r})$ . This can be ascribed to the decoration of Au NPs on DUT-67 $(\boldsymbol{Z}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{r}})$ , which has been discussed above. It is worth noticing that no product is yielded without using catalysts or without light irradiation, and photocatalytic performance dramatically decreases in the atmosphere of $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ . Therefore, a catalyst, light sources and $0_{2}$ are all of necessity for the reaction. No product can be detected when the temperature increases up to $80~^{\circ}C$ in dark condition, suggesting that the reaction is a photocatalytic process. As a contrast, $\mathsf{A u@U i O–66(Z r)}$ shows much lower photocatalytic activity with only the conversion of $8.4\%$ , indicating that DUT-67(Zr) as the support plays a critical part in this process. The photocatalytic performance tests with prolonged reaction time over $\mathsf{A u@D U T-67(Z r)}$ were carried out as Fig. S5. With the reaction time prolong from $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ to $^{10\mathrm{~h~}}$ , the conversion gradually increases up to $100\%$ . The selectivity drops slightly, but still maintains above $94\%$ By comparing with reported photocatalysts in Table S2, $\mathsf{A u@D U T-67(Z r)}$ shows the comparable photocatalytic performance for the oxidation of benzylamine.
The universal applicability of Au@DUT-67(Zr) was tested for the amines with different groups ${\mathrm{R}}=-{\mathsf{O C H}}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{-CH}_{3}$ , -Cl, $\begin{array}{r}{-\mathsf{C F}_{3}.}\end{array}$ ) in Table 1. Au@DUT-67(Zr) can availably transfer the amine to the matching imine with high selectivity $(>99\%)$ . Notably, Au@DUT-67(Zr) shows a higher conversion for the amines with stronger electrondonating groups $(-0\mathsf{C H_{3}}>-\mathsf{C H_{3}}>-\mathsf{H})$ but a lower conversion for the amines with stronger electron-withdrawing groups $(\mathrm{-}\mathrm{CF}_{3}<\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}$ ${\mathsf{I}}<-{\mathsf{H}}_{,}^{\mathsf{}}$ than that for benzylamine. Furthermore, after the recycling of $\mathsf{A u@D U T-67(Z r)}$ for four times in Fig. S6, its photocatalytic performance remains stable, suggesting the excellent reusability of $\mathsf{A u@D U T-67(Z r)}$ . The XRD pattern (Fig. S7) of used Au@DUT-67 $(\boldsymbol{Z}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{r}})$ after four recycles shows an identical pattern to that of fresh sample, suggesting the high stability of Au@DUT-67(Zr). The intensity of FTIR peaks (Fig. S8) for $\mathsf{A u@D U T-67(Z r)}$ is not reduced after visible light for $^{4\mathrm{~h~}}$ , indicating that $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{T D C}$ linkers would not be consumed. The peaks ascribed to $-\mathsf{C O O}^{-}$ and Zr-(OC) show no shift, suggesting that visible light cannot induce the decarboxylation of ligands to form the VO. Therefore, Au@DUT-67(Zr) possesses the good stability under visible light.
In order to optimize reaction conditions, Au loading amount and reaction temperature were also considered. In Fig. S9, by increasing the mass fraction of Au from $0.5\%$ to $2.0\%$ , the conversion increases initially and decreases afterwards. And optimum loading amount of Au is $1.0\%$ . As reported previously, too low loading amount of metal NPs results in insignificant multiple scattering and thus no obvious increase for the light absorption. Nevertheless, excessive loading level leads to many photon absorption at the first contact to metal NPs, causing a small amount of photons for multiple scattering [55]. It must be mentioned that the reaction temperature can affect the photocatalytic activity (Fig. S10). By slightly elevating reaction temperature, the conversion observably increased. When reaction temperature rose to $55~^{\circ}C$ the high conversion $(93.5~\%)$ with the high selectivity $(95.1\ \%)$ was achieved over Au@DUT-67(Zr). The main reason is that the separation of hot electrons from Au NPs is facilitated by elevating reaction temperature.
## 3.3. Photocatalytic mechanism
In situ FTIR tests were performed to explore the adsorption of BA on the catalyst. At physical and chemical adsorption stage (Fig. 6A (3), Fig. 6B (3)), Au@DUT-67(Zr) appears some characteristic peaks ascribed to BA. At chemical adsorption stage (Fig. 6A (4), Fig. 6B (4)), after a further evacuation, these characteristic peaks retain, indicating BA is effectually chemisorbed on Au@DUT-67 (Zr). Interestingly, the peaks for $3366~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $3292~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , assigned to the asymmetrical stretching vibration $(\upsigma_{\mathrm{as}})$ and the symmetrical stretching vibration $(\upsigma_{s})$ of $\boldsymbol{-}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{N}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{H}}_{2}$ , shift toward lower wavenumbers $3349~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $3266~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ), suggesting the $\mathsf{N{\mathrm{-}}H}$ bond is weaken and electron cloud density of the bond is reduced. Meanwhile, the peak $\cdot1453\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ) for bending vibration (d) of $\mathrm{-CH}_{2}$ also shows a negative shift $(1451~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ ), revealing that $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{H}$ bonds for $\mathrm{-CH}_{2}$ become weaker and the electron cloud density becomes less. Nevertheless, the peaks for $3085~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $3061~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $3027~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which are ascribed to ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{H}}$ bonds for aromatic ring, show no move compared with that of free BA molecules. It is known that the electronegativity of N atoms is stronger than that of C atoms, thus N atoms have a stronger attraction of electrons than C atoms. Therefore, the migration of electrons due to LMCT might take place via the pathway depicted in Fig. 6C. BA molecules are chemisorbed and activated on the coordinatively unsaturated Zr sites of $\mathsf{A u@D U T-67(Z r)}$ via ${\mathrm{H}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{N}}^{\cdot\cdot}{\mathsf{Z}}{\mathrm{r}}$ coordination species. The electrons migrate from C atoms of $-\mathsf{C H}_{2}$ to N atoms of $\boldsymbol{\mathbf{\ell}}-\boldsymbol{\mathrm{NH}}_{2}$ , and then the electrons subsequently migrate from N atoms of $\boldsymbol{-}\mathsf{N}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ to the coordinatively unsaturated $Z\mathrm{r}$ atoms. This pathway could accelerate the deprotonation of BA by losing a H of $-\mathsf{C H}_{2}$ [56].
The ESR coupled with DMPO was proceeded to determine active species for this photocatalytic pathway. For Fig. 6D, no signal was observed in the darkness. Under visible light, ESR spectrum of Au@DUT-67(Zr) obviously exhibits the typical sextet signal, confirming the presence of superoxide radical $(\mathbf{\cdot}0_{2}^{-})$ [57]. However, $\mathsf{A u@U i O–66(Z r)}$ shows a much weaker signal of $\cdot_{0_{2}^{-}}$ . As explained in reported studies [51,58], VO can not only serve as the active site to capture plasmonic hot electrons, but also provide the site to adsorb and activate $0_{2}$ . Hence, $\cdot_{0_{2}^{-}}$ was formed because of the reduction of oxygen molecules by hot electrons captured on VO. It was revealed in Fig. 4A that Au@DUT-67(Zr) shows a stronger ESR signal of VO than Au@UiO-66(Zr), indicating that Au@DUT$67(\mathrm{Zr})$ possesses more VO. Therefore, Au@DUT-67(Zr) possesses a better capacity for the reduction of $0_{2}$ molecules to $\cdot_{0_{2}^{-}}$ radicals, which greatly contributes to improving the performance on the selective oxidation.
According to the above findings, a photocatalytic mechanism is presented to illustrate the selective oxidation of BA in Scheme 2. Initially, BA and $0_{2}$ molecules will efficiently be chemisorbed and activated on coordinatively unsaturated Zr atoms in Au@DUT-67 $(\mathrm{Zr})$ via $\mathrm{H}{-}\mathsf{N}{\cdot}\mathsf{Z}\mathrm{r}$ and $0^{...}Z\mathrm{r}$ , respectively. Meanwhile, the process achieves the initial deprotonation of BA to form BA cation by losing a H of $\mathsf{-C H}_{2}$ due to LMCT (Step 1: The activation of BA and $\begin{array}{r}{{{0}_{2}}.}\end{array}$ ). Under visible light, Au NPs are excited to yield plasmonic hot electrons, which are instantly captured by VO to reduce the adsorbed and activated $0_{2}$ to form $\cdot0_{2}^{-}$ (Step 2: The forming of active species). Assisted with $\cdot_{0_{2}^{-}}$ , BA cation is further deprotonated to form benzaldehyde by plasmonic hot holes (Step 3: The attack of active species). Finally, target product (BE) is produced by the easy nucleophilic attack of benzaldehyde with the unreacted BA molecules (Step 4: The trigger of the coupled reaction). The successful release of reagent molecules and $0_{2}$ molecules from active sites in $\mathsf{A u@D U T-67(Z r)}$ permits the next circulation (Step 5: The next activeation).
## 4. Conclusion
We successfully fabricated Au nanoparticles(NPs)-decorated defective DUT-67(Zr) MOF $(\mathsf{A u@D U T-67(Z r)}).$ containing oxygen vacancies (VO) for selective photocatalytic transformation of amine to imine driven by visible light, which showed 7.5 times higher conversion than Au NPs-decorated UiO-66(Zr) $\left(\mathsf{A u@U i O-}\right.$ $66(Z\mathrm{r}))$ and also showed a better performance than some reported MOF-based photocatalysts [59–61]. The partially hollow structure of DUT-67(Zr) ensures that Au NPs can be effectively fixed on DUT$67(\mathrm{Zr})$ and more active sites including coordinatively unsaturated
Zr atom and VO are formed on DUT-67(Zr). The unsaturated Zr sites facilitate activating benzylamine molecules by the deprotonation of $-\mathsf{C H}_{2}$ . VO enhance the reduction of activated oxygen $(0_{2})$ to superoxide radical $(\cdot0_{2}^{-})$ by plasmonic hot electrons. Assisted with $\bullet0_{2}^{-}$ , plasmonic hot holes effectively promote the selective oxidation reaction. This work has not only developed an innovative and facile strategy to design effective MOF-based photocatalysts, but also proposed a deeper understanding of the photocatalytic mechanism relying on the molecule activation on catalyst interfaces.
